Have you considering using the following string to fire it up:
./setiathome -nice 15 -nolock -proxy 66.28.250.122 > /dev/null &

The IP is the actual Seti WU [work unit] server. You may wish to use
the -QUIT  flag in order to close the program.  Personally I believe they
specified that it should be shutdown in that manner for a reason. With
SIGQUIT, the program basically closes itself at its own pace instead of
being forced to exit in the middle of a work unit and that may be the cause
of your problem.

Forcing it to terminate may be corrupting the data that it has stored in
memory. That 300k work unit, when being processed, requires about 16mb of
ram. AKA a lot of processing.

As for crashing when it can't connect, you're right. It doesn't retry, it
simply closes. What I did for that was, create a cron job that shuts down
and restarts seti every 2hrs...so that if there's an issue connecting to the
seti server, setiathome will retry every 2hrs.

