Måns Rullgård wrote:
> Lamar Owen <lowen@pari.edu> writes:
>> The initial reboot into the fully installed system after stage 3 on my
>> AS2100 spewed machine checks.  Took way too long to build for way too
>> little benefit.
 
> I guess you just had bad luck.  My transition from a degrading
> Slackware to Gentoo was smooth.

Nah, I had the 'generic kernel SMP gives Sable heartburn' problem. I did the
Gentoo build before knowing about the need to do Sable-specific SMP
configs. But the whole process was so drawn out that I had time to download
all 7 Debian ISOs and make a full mirror of the Debian/Alpha and
Debian/SPARC trees.  In contrast, after popping in the Debian disc, typing
'boot dka600 -flags 0', I had a working Debian in about an hour.  Gentoo
from Stage 3 took many many hours, most of which was kernel rebuild.  A
stage 1 would have been much more painful, for absolutely no gain
whatsoever.

FWIW, I have pulled down the AlphaCore stuff and plan on trying the newest
1.0a once ISO's are available.  AlphaCore 0.9 throws errors during the
installation, complaining about fatal errors during installation of random
RPMs.  AC 0.9 works great on my 164SX, but not on the Sable.
 
>> Got Debian on the box now and it's happy.

> I avoid running Debian, since I disagree with much of their policy.
> IMNSHO, Debian is 30% ideology, 20% religion, and 50% OS.  I prefer at
> least 80% OS on my machines.

But Gentoo is less than 50% OS too, because you have the ideology (build
from SOURCE mantra), religion (build from ONLY the SOURCE!), and lots and
lots of expensive time spent doing things that an installation routine
should do for you.  Things like configuring /etc/aboot.conf properly. 
Things like setting up SRM to autoboot.  Things like properly configuring
the kernel for your hardware. You know, small things like that.  The Gentoo
installation routine is horrid; I'd be just as well off doing a Linux From
Scratch install.  And there is virtually NO GAIN to be found in doing it
that way.  One can do the very same thing with a Debian or Redhat-derived
install by building from the source packages.  Rebuild instructions are
very plentiful for these, and you can optimize to your heart's content and
STILL have a decent installer.  Of course, installation is only supposed to
be done once, but, in practice, that isn't the case.

I'm going to laugh the first time Gentoo has a flag day due to a binary
incompatible glibc change, or something like going to udev that is going to
be virtually impossible to do in the emerge environment: meaning a complete
reinstallation is going to be required.  That will be very painful.  

Even the BSD's with their ports systems have flag days, across which there
are no upgrades possible, using ports and build from source.  

FWIW, I am no stranger to these issues, having been the lead RPM maintainer
for the PostgreSQL Development Group since 1999.  I'm a little new to
Alphas, but am far from new to Linux, having had a live 24/7 server setup
streaming audio since 1997, and an internet presence since 1991.
