Well, that is certainly a tradeoff.  On one hand, we have the time spent
building the system.  On the other hand, we have hard drive space.  My
question is 'which is less expensive?' (My time is more expensive than hard
disk space any day).  You certainly have more choices, and more
responsibilities.  I prefer to put a more simply administered system into
place where I can redo the install if needed quickly, I can add packages
easily without any recompile (suppose you have USE set for non GNOME, and
then you need, say, GNUcash for a user....)  Like everything else, a
tradeoff.  I'd rather spend a little more drive space (since I have the
space to spend) and less time (which I never have enough of) using Debian
