Atro Tossavainen <Atro.Tossavainen+news@helsinki.finland.invalid> wrote:
> John Beardmore <wookie@wookie.demon.co.uk> writes:
> 
>>         1567085  Feb 15 08:04    vmlinux-2.4.29.gz
>> 
>> Not exactly huge.
> 
> Shouldn't be an issue.
> 
> # ls -la /boot/*29*
> -rw-r--r--    1 root     root      1453207 Jan 27 08:59 /boot/vmlinuz-2.4.29-superpage.gz

A size of gzip compressed image gives you only a very rough
"guesstimate" of what will really use a memory in initial stages and
how.  A boot loader unpacks that into a set aside area of memory and
passes execution there.  If something which should not be touched, and
that includes what is expected by SRM, got clobbered in that process
then you are in trouble.  Have a look at an aboot code how execution
starts in a kernel. This is not that complicated.

OTOH errors in a boot configuration (not that device for a root file
system, a root file system on an inaccessible device or of a file type
which will be supported only "later", etc.) are vastly more popular. :-)
Errors in a setup code, especially for a hardware with relatively few
owners, are also not unheard of.  Alphas have a lot of "interesting"
hardware/firmware combinations with documentation not exactly abundant.

  Michal


