In article <0rgkbesLws$BFwDn@wookie.demon.co.uk>,
John Beardmore  <wookie@wookie.demon.co.uk> wrote:
>Alpha Debian v3r4 2.2.20-generic, ps -fe command works, but starts its 
>response with
>
>   "Unknown HZ value! (341) Assume 1024."
>
>
>Should I worry ?  A quick google seems to suggest this is an old 
>problem.

I tried for a while to find a fix, and decided it was not worth it.
The problem seems to be self-healing with time.  There is a 32-bit
overflow somewhere in code that counts up ticks of the 1024-Hz clock.
I think it has a repetition period of about 48 days.  2^32 / 1024 / 86400.

I myself have only noticed this after long uptime, exceeding 180 days.
Here are a few lines from my "UnknownHZ" log:

Fri Jul 30 11:18:44 PDT 2004
Unknown HZ value! (1285) Assume 1024.
 11:16:36 up 189 days, 22:41,  3 users,  load average: 0.45, 0.41, 0.47
Mon Aug 30 23:01:10 PDT 2004
Unknown HZ value! (1248) Assume 1024.
 23:01:18 up 221 days, 10:25,  3 users,  load average: 0.97, 0.50, 0.39
Sun Sep 12 13:55:58 PDT 2004
Unknown HZ value! (1236) Assume 1024.
 13:56:05 up 234 days,  1:28,  3 users,  load average: 0.01, 0.06, 0.14
Tue Sep 14 11:35:57 PDT 2004
 11:36:08 up 235 days, 23:08,  3 users,  load average: 0.28, 0.45, 0.58

Notice that the problem went away by itself some 46 days after I noticed
it last July.  Shortly after that, we had a power outage, and I had to
start accumulating uptime all over again.

This is on an Alpha DS-10 running Debian version 3.0 with kernel 2.2.20.

    carl
-- 
