> and mysql (the forum system for example) are running quiet well.

You'd have to look at what Apache is doing.  That may be quite difficult.
Not knowing anything about the application, it's hard to say more.  Does
it behave in anything like the same way on x86?

> Isn't it possible to build a web cluster with apache, running on axp?

It should be.  A quick look at mod_backhand and LVS documentation
doesn't reveal anything x86 specific to me.

> If node 1 is busy, than use node 2, if node 2 is busy, use node 3 ...
> But this isn't really what i want.
> 
> I just want, that the load of one CPU is splitted onto 3 CPUs (for
> example) through a network connection. (The Cluster should work like a
> big SMP system)

As I see it, you need kernel-level load balancing for this.  The only
software that does this in any way is MOSIX and OpenMosix, and they can
only split work that can be divided into individually forked processes
onto several CPUs.  Neither package supports Alpha.  You can't do what
you want to do at the moment, and given that Alpha is on its way out,
it is unlikely that either MOSIX or OpenMosix folks will focus their
efforts into porting to the Alpha.

