> http://kcnetwork.net/tmp/xfce-4.2/]. The gap is such, programs pretty much
> have to be written with CCC in mind or they simply don't compile. One of the
> few mainstream programs around that do that is Apache, and I'm not sure they
> still bother to do it. Will L G

You have it backwards. gcc compiles more software than ccc because it has
significant, non-ANSI-standard extensions, while ccc follows the ANSI
standard and adds some gcc extenstions. Lots of software out there is
written assuming that gcc is the only compiler in the world, especially in
the Linux world. It's not ccc's fault that people out there write
non-standards-conformant programs. Programs don't have to be written with
ccc in mind; they simply have to be written to the ANSI standard, not gcc's
extensions.

This is the same kind of thinking that leads people to write code, again
mainly in the Linux world, assuming that it'll never run on anything but a
Pentium. Alpha programmers should know better than to make that kind of
platform-specific assumption.


