John Beardmore <wookie@wookie.demon.co.uk> writes:

> Trouble is, I don't know which modes will work.  I can guess the 
> resolutions, but I've no idea about refresh rates etc.

You wouldn't have any XFree86.0.log's from the old Red Hat 6.2 system
hanging around?  Or even the /etc/X11/XF86Config?

You should be able to pick a monitor in the autoconfiguration tool.
Getting the monitor specs right will prevent you from attempting rates
that the monitor can't cope with.  In XF86 4.x, the necessary modelines
would be auto-created by the server on the fly, in 3.x, the xserver-s3
Debian package should have come with a sample /etc/X11/XF86Config that
has all the necessary modelines.  Then just pick the depths and modes
you want in this section of the XF86Config:

Section "Screen"
    Driver      "accel"
    Device      "Matrox Millennium G200"
    Monitor     "Sony Multiscan 500 PS"
    Subsection "Display"
        Depth       8
        Modes       "640x480" "800x600" "1024x768" "1280x1024"
        ViewPort    0 0
    EndSubsection
EndSection

The modes and depths you can use are obviously dependent on the amount
of VRAM the card has (probably either 2M or 4M, corresponding to max
resolutions of 800*600 at 24 bit depth, 1024*768 at 16 bit, 1280*1024
at 8 bit (for 2M) or 1280*1024 at 16 bit and anything else at 24 for
4M).

